There are 52 adjacent pairs: count each position together with its clockwise neighbor. For any such pair, the probability that the first card is black is \(26/52\). Given that it is black, 25 of the remaining 51 cards are black, so the probability that both are black is \((26/52)(25/51)\).
The number of black-black pairs is the sum of the 52 indicators for these events. By linearity of expectation, its mean is \(52(26/52)(25/51)=650/51\).
Final answer: \frac{650}{51}
